README

This sample program is only working on Raspberry Pi models with an on-board
Ethernet controller (or a supported USB Ethernet device) and an SD card or
on-board eMMC memory (Compute Module 5).

It runs a small HTTP web server which lets you do the following from a web
browser:

	1. Set any of the exposed GPIO pins of the 40-pin header to a HIGH or
	   LOW output level.

	2. Output a PWM waveform with a selectable duty cycle (0% to 100%) on
	   multiple GPIO pins, using two different generators:

	   a) Hardware PWM (high frequency, 1 kHz to 20 MHz). This uses the
	      hardware PWM peripheral of the SoC. It has exactly two channels,
	      each wired to a fixed pin:

		Channel 1: GPIO12 (Alt0) or GPIO18 (Alt5)
		Channel 2: GPIO13 (Alt0) or GPIO19 (Alt5)

	      Both channels share a single frequency but have an independent
	      duty cycle. Near the top of the frequency range the duty cycle
	      resolution becomes coarse, because the PWM clock has to run at
	      (frequency x range) and the range (number of duty steps) shrinks
	      as the frequency rises. The web page reports the frequency that
	      was actually generated, which can differ slightly from the value
	      you requested (the clock is derived by an integer divider from a
	      fixed source clock).

	   b) Software PWM (1 Hz to 100 kHz) on any of the other header pins,
	      each with its own frequency and duty cycle. Any number of pins
	      can run software PWM at the same time.

	3. Read and write the SD card (or the on-board eMMC memory on a
	   Compute Module 5): browse the directories, upload new files,
	   download files back to your computer and delete files.

	4. Reboot the Raspberry Pi.

The software PWM is generated using the class CUserTimer (see sample/36-softpwm),
which fires a fine grained timer interrupt (via the FIQ). A single handler
schedules the next edge across all active software PWM pins and toggles them.
The time base is 1 microsecond (USER_CLOCKHZ), so the software PWM is limited to
lower frequencies (capped at 100 kHz) and, being interrupt driven, has some
jitter which grows with the frequency and the number of active pins. For clean,
high frequency output use the hardware PWM channels described above.

The hardware PWM is driven by the class CPWMOutput (see sample/13-pwmoutput) in
M/S mode. This sample selects the clock source, integer divider and range
automatically from the requested frequency. It is available on the Raspberry Pi
1 to 4 only; on the Raspberry Pi 5 (RP1) only the software PWM is available.

WARNING: A PWM clock near 100 MHz (needed to reach 20 MHz output) is beyond the
officially specified range of the PWM peripheral. The output may be less clean
at the very highest frequencies. For a very clean fixed-frequency 50% clock on
the dedicated clock pins, see the class CGPIOClock and sample/11-gpioclock.

The storage device is accessed with the FatFs file system (addon/fatfs) on top
of the EMMC driver (addon/SDCard). Long file names are supported. The directory
tree can be browsed, starting from the root directory of the first FAT partition
of the device.

Compute Module 5
----------------

On a Compute Module 5 (not the CM5 Lite) the on-board eMMC memory is used
instead of an SD card. It is connected to the same SDHCI controller of the
BCM2712 SoC, which drives the SD card on the Raspberry Pi 5. The CM5 is detected
at run-time with CEMMCDevice::GetDefaultDeviceForMachine(), so the same kernel
image works on a Raspberry Pi 5 with SD card and on a CM5 with eMMC memory. The
system option USE_EMBEDDED_MMC_CM (see include/circle/sysconfig.h) is required
for the Compute Module 3+ and 4 only.

Build this sample with AARCH = 64 and RASPPI = 5 for the CM5. To install it,
put the CM5 into the USB boot mode (jumper "nRPI_BOOT" on the IO board) and
attach the eMMC memory as a USB mass-storage device to your PC using the tool
"rpiboot" from [https://github.com/raspberrypi/usbboot]. Then copy the firmware
files (see boot/), config64.txt (renamed to config.txt) and kernel_2712.img to
the FAT partition ("bootfs") of the eMMC memory.

Only FAT partitions can be read. A Raspberry Pi OS installation has its root
file system on an ext4 partition, which is not supported by Circle. It will be
listed in the system log by the partition manager (as "emmc1-2"), but its files
cannot be accessed.

Before building this sample you need to build the two add-on libraries it uses:

	make -C ../../addon/SDCard
	make -C ../../addon/fatfs

You may also have to update the network configuration (DHCP is used by default)
in kernel.cpp to meet your local network configuration. Don't forget to connect
your local Ethernet to the Ethernet connector of the Raspberry Pi.

First the Act LED blinks 5 times to show the program has started. After
initialization the program is waiting for incoming TCP connections on port 80
(http). The IP address to use is printed on the screen and the serial log. Open
that URL ("http://<ip-address>/") in your web browser on another computer on
your network.

The controllable pins are the usual header GPIOs in Broadcom (BCM) numbering,
except GPIO14 and GPIO15, which are used by the serial port for the system log.

Notes and limitations:

	- File downloads are limited to the content buffer size (see
	  MAX_CONTENT_SIZE in webserver.cpp). Uploads are limited by
	  MAX_UPLOAD_SIZE.

	- At most MAX_FILE_ENTRIES (see webserver.cpp) entries are shown per
	  directory.

	- The storage device is normally the same device the program was booted
	  from. Deleting or overwriting the firmware or kernel files will make
	  the Raspberry Pi (or Compute Module) unbootable, so be careful which
	  files you remove. This is especially important for the eMMC memory of
	  a Compute Module, which cannot be simply replaced like an SD card.

WARNING: Driving GPIO pins connects them directly to the Raspberry Pi SoC. Make
sure that whatever you connect to a pin is safe to drive (3.3V logic, no shorts
to 5V or GND on an output pin). You use this at your own risk.
